(project pricklypear-branching
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: branching")
 (status partial)
 (* agent note (|2026-08-02 status reconcile: agent-promote, package-updates, branch-browser-ui → implemented. Still open: schema-authority (type_defs merge), ast-nodes (design).|) *)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section branching
  (doc "Branches as the fundamental organizational primitive: code branches, shared data, fork/merge")
  (status partial)
  
  (details
   (doc (|
        Branches are to Pricklypear what files are to
        Unix: the fundamental organizational primitive.
        Everything is a branch; everything else is built
        on top of that.

        STORAGE (one Postgres, not one DB per branch):
        - branches(id, parent_id, owner, name, ...)
        - function_defs / type_defs / view_bindings
          each keyed by (branch_id, name|route)
        - data_rows has NO branch_id — shared data
        No separate schemas/tables per branch. No
        Postgres INHERITS (see storage.no-inheritance).

        KEY DISTINCTION: branches are for CODE, not data.
        - BRANCHED: function definitions, type schemas,
          view/route bindings.
        - SHARED: data_rows (todos, notes, bible, …).
          Alice's experimental branch renders against
          REAL shared data. She forks code, not data.

        TWO FORK SHAPES:
        - EMPTY child (personal garden): parent pointer
          only; inheritance via recursive CTE. Main
          package updates stay visible unless overridden.
        - FULL fork (experiment): copy code-rows from
          parent (cheap snapshot). Parent updates do not
          appear until merge.

        ROLES:
          system/main     package defaults (inheritance root)
          <user>/personal garden (boot default; long-lived pins)
          <user>/dev      optional workshop (empty child of main;
                          active product work intended to promote)
          <user>/<name>   experiments (full fork when isolation
                          from evolving main is required)

        AUTHORITY (Nopales vs kernel) — see package-updates:
          Kernel (OCaml/migrations)  → git is source of truth
          Package Nopales               → Postgres branches are live
                                       source; git holds snapshots
          Boot seed                  → additive into system/main
                                       only (never clobber existing)
          Explicit reseed            → git libs may overwrite main
                                       when an admin asks

        Effective definition lookup (CLOS override):
        walk parent chain, closest definition wins.
        Writes (define/route) always hit the ACTIVE
        branch only.
   |))
  )
  (subsection fork
   (doc "Forking a branch")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         (fork 'main 'alice/experiment)

         Implementation:
         1. INSERT INTO branches (id, parent_id, owner,
            name, ...) VALUES (new_uuid, main_id, 'alice',
            'experiment', ...)
         2. INSERT INTO function_defs (branch_id, name,
            body, author, updated_at)
            SELECT new_uuid, name, body, author,
            updated_at FROM function_defs
            WHERE branch_id = parent_id
         3. Similarly for type_defs, view_bindings

         Cost: O(num code rows in parent). For ~100 fns
         and ~20 types, a few hundred row inserts. Lands
         in well under a second.

         Forking does NOT copy data table rows. The
         forked branch's functions operate on the same
         shared event rows as the parent.
   |))
   )
   (verify
    (test "image/test/primitives_test.ml — 48 alcotest cases")
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "fork creates a branch and copies function_defs")
    (smoke "override-walk resolves child overrides before parent; branches isolate writes"))

  )
  (subsection merge
   (doc "Merging a branch into its parent")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         (merge 'alice/experiment 'alice/main)

         Policy:
         - Default: admin-only merges to main,
           per-user can merge into their own branches
           freely.
         - Expandable later (PR/review flow).

         Implementation (Store.merge_branch, image/lib/store.ml):
         upserts all function_defs rows from source into target
         via INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (branch_id, name) DO UPDATE,
         wrapped in a SERIALIZABLE transaction. Initial cut
         copies the full set; diff-based merge (only changed
         rows, per-function conflict resolution) is not yet
         implemented.

         CONFLICT REPORT (v1: report-only, never blocks):
         before the upsert, merge computes names where BOTH
         source owns a row AND target owns a row whose
         updated_at is newer than source's (target moved
         independently since the lens diverged). Result
         string carries them:
           "merged N rows (M conflicts: a, b, c)"
         Resolution stays last-write-wins (source body lands);
         the report tells the human what was clobbered, and
         code-history makes the clobbered target body
         recoverable. Blocking/require-resolution merge is a
         later PR/review-flow concern, not v1.

         SCOPE: merge copies function_defs only. It MUST NOT
         copy type_defs (schema authority is main; see
         schema-authority) and MUST NOT copy view_bindings
         blindly (route hijack risk). Routes travel with
         named functions through promote instead
         (see promote).

         type_defs merge: not implemented by design in v1.
         A branch cannot propose a schema change through merge;
         schema changes land via migrations applied on boot
         (see migrations.additive-only) and are visible to all
         branches on next lookup.
   |))
   )
   (verify
    (test "image/test/primitives_test.ml — fork/merge/promote alcotest cases")
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "merge upserts changed rows; merges into system/* require admin")
    (smoke "merge result string lists conflict names when target moved newer")
    (smoke "merge copies no type_defs and no view_bindings rows"))
  )
  (subsection diff
   (doc "Structural divergence between two branches (AST-aware, not textual)")
   (status implemented)
   (implements image/lib/store.ml image/lib/prims_db.ml image/lib/http_branches.ml lisp/lib/ast_diff.ml lisp/test/ast_diff_test.ml)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-16 ralph loop 19: implemented + verified. Store.diff_branches (store.ml), (diff from to) prim (prims_db.ml), GET /diff?from=&to= private page (http_branches.ml, http.ml route) wired; ast_diff 27/27 green, full dune build @all + runtest exit 0. 2026-08-14: gap surfaced testing personal-vs-main divergence. merge_branch is naive full-copy with explicit TODOs (diff-based merge, conflict detection); diff is the primitive those TODOs consume. AST-awareness chosen because function_defs.body is ONE expr node (not a statement list), JSONB-tagged structurally - see lisp-interpreter.ast-diff.|) *)
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (test "lisp/test/ast_diff_test.ml — same/modified over all 11 real expr constructors (Var Lit App Let Letrec If Lambda Prim Seq Quote Define)")
    (test "App.args and Letrec.bindings use list-LCS keyed on analyze.ast_hash so insertion mid-sequence does not mark every following element changed")
    (test "top-level ast_hash equality short-circuits the walk for unchanged rows")
    (smoke "(diff 'system/main 'admin/personal) lists added/removed/modified names without scraping /bindings HTML")
    (smoke "/diff?from=system/main&to=admin/personal renders Modified rows with per-ctor AST deltas; Added/Removed show pp_expr of the lone body"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         PROBLEM: testing divergence between a personal
         branch and main is currently manual —
         (list-bindings) under each (checkout ...) and
         eyeballing. merge_branch (see merge) copies ALL
         function_defs source->target with three open
         TODOs: diff-based merge (only changed rows),
         conflict detection (parent moved same name
         since fork), and per-function resolution. diff
         is the primitive those TODOs consume, and the
         thing a human/agent reads before promoting.

         (diff 'system/main 'admin/personal)
           -> VList of change-dicts, one per divergent
              name (no row for unchanged — the common
              case):
              {name kind status from-updated to-updated}
              kind   :: fn | type | route
              status :: added | removed | modified

         LAYERING (two stages, at the seam the codebase
         already has):
           1. ROW CLASSIFICATION (SQL, one round trip):
              both effective views via the SAME recursive
              CTE that list_function_names_meta /
              list_packages / search_function_bodies use
              (closest-ancestor-wins). FULL OUTER JOIN on
              name, classify each as
                added    = in to,   not in from
                removed  = in from, not in to
                modified = both, AND from.body IS DISTINCT
                                       FROM to.body
              Scope mirrors merge_branch: function_defs +
              type_defs + view_bindings (NOT data_rows —
              shared, branch-free per branching.data).
              Conflicts fall out for free: this is the
              inverted conflict_sql already in merge_branch.
           2. AST DELTA (OCaml, Modified rows ONLY):
              parse both bodies via expr_of_json, walk with
              lisp/lib/ast_diff.ml (see lisp-interpreter.
              ast-diff). Never runs on Added/Removed
              (nothing to compare — show pp_expr of the
              lone body) or on Same (short-circuited).

         AST-AWARENESS IS A PROPERTY, NOT A NICETY.
         function_defs.body is ONE expr node (Lambda body,
         not a statement list), JSONB-tagged structurally
         ({"if":...}, {"lambda":...}, {"app":...}). A
         textual diff over the JSONB ::text would be
         unreadable; a line diff over pp_expr output
         would jitter on whitespace and miss structural
         moves. The diff is structural over the expr type:

           ctor differs (Var -> Lambda)            = Replaced
           same ctor, fixed-arity children          = Node,
             recurse positionally (If test/then/else;    each
             Seq a/b; Lambda params/body; Let name/      child
             val/body; Define name/val; Quote body;       its
             Var s; Lit v)                                own
                                                        delta
           same ctor, list children                = Node
             with a list-LCS step over the children      plus a
             before recursing into matched pairs:        moved/
             - App.args (head + args list)              added
             - Letrec.bindings (keyed on binding name)  list
                                                        handled
           leaf value differs (Var s, Lit v)        = Replaced

         The 11 real expr constructors (ast.ml): Var,
         Lit, App, Let, Letrec, If, Lambda, Prim, Seq,
         Quote, Define. The Quasiquote / Unquote /
         UnquoteSplicing mentioned in lisp-interpreter.
         ast are reader-level sugar that desugars before
         persistence (see macros) — they do not appear
         in function_defs.body and need no diff case.

         FAST PATH: analyze.ast_hash (sha1_hex of
         Reader.pp_expr) is already the content identity.
         Compare from/to hashes first; if equal, skip the
         walk entirely (returns Same). The 90% case
         (unchanged defs between branches) costs one hash
         compare per row, not a tree walk.

         SCOPE & TYPE/ROUTE HANDLING:
           - function_defs: full AST diff as above.
           - type_defs: diff the `fields` jsonb array
             with the same list-LCS (field declarations
             are positional lists, not expr trees; no
             ast_diff involvement).
           - view_bindings: tabular (route, handler)
             pair sets — divergent pairs are
             added/removed rows, no body to diff. Stays
             in SQL.
           Schema changes land via migrations (see
           schema-authority), never via diff/merge.

         NON-GOALS (v1, honest):
           - No rename detection. A bound-var rename
             x->y surfaces as a Replaced at the binding
             site (Lambda.params / Let name / Letrec
             binding name) AND a Replaced at every
             reference site (Var x -> Var y). git doesn't
             do this for free either; real rename
             detection needs the 3-way base (fork point),
             which is the natural v2 once code_history
             before-images + branches.last_synced are
             wired as the base.
           - No alpha-equivalence. Bound names are NOT
             normalized before comparing — a genuine
             rename is meant to be visible, not hidden.
           - Param order matters. (lambda (a b) ...) vs
             (lambda (b a) ...) is a positional child
             delta, NOT "renamed params".

         DEGRADED RENDER PATH: if the /diff page does
         not want to render the structured delta tree,
         fall back to a unified line diff over
         Reader.pp_expr from / Reader.pp_expr to. This is
         the existing simple_text_diff shape (see
         code-diff) applied to two live bodies instead
         of a history entry. Always available; never the
         primary surface.

         WIRE-IN:
           - Store.diff_branches ~from_id ~to_id
             (image/lib/store.ml, beside merge_branch):
             the SQL stage + Modified-row (from_expr,
             to_expr) pairs ready for the image layer.
           - (diff source target) prim (image/lib/
             prims_db.ml, beside code-diff): returns the
             VList of change-dicts (summary level). The
             structured AST delta is consumed in-process
             by the /diff page renderer (Ast_html +
             Ast_diff), NOT exposed as a separate prim
             in v1. A programmatic (diff-ast name from
             to) prim is a v2 addition if agents need
             the delta tree as Nopales values.
           - /diff?from=&to= private page (image/lib/
             http_branches.ml, sibling to /bindings):
             three-column row table; Modified rows
             expand to the AST delta overlaying
             Ast_html.pp_expr_html. Future "promote
             selected" off diff rows IS the merge TODO
             (diff-based merge) in disguise.

         DOWNSTREAM CONSUMERS (the point of building it):
           - merge TODO "diff-based merge (only changed
             rows)": merge calls diff, upserts only rows
             whose status != same.
           - merge TODO "conflict detection": diff's
             Modified classification + 3-way base
             (code_history before-image at
             last_synced) yields genuine conflict
             detection. v2.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection agent-promote
   (doc "Agent promotes from its workshop into the parent human's personal")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02: promote_to_personal + allow_agent_promote user toggle + set-allow-agent-promote prim; agent boots parent/agent workshop.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         promote(source=<parent>/agent, target=<parent>/personal):
         Selective upsert of function_defs from the agent's
         workshop into the parent human's personal branch (same
         mechanism as promote_to_main, different target). The
         agent's work lands in the human's garden.

         Permission: gated by the parent human's allow_agent_promote
         toggle (users table, default true). The human can revoke
         it to freeze the agent out of their personal branch; the
         agent still works in <parent>/agent but cannot promote.

         This is the human-in-the-loop gate: the agent builds in
         its own branch; nothing reaches the shared tree
         (system/main) or the human's garden until the agent
         promotes and the human allows it. promote to system/main
         remains admin-only.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection schema-authority
   (doc "Types in main, branches read against main's schema")
   (status partial)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02 confirm partial: shared data_rows + additive migrations; type_defs merge still not implemented (details admit).|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Schema authority is 'main'. Branches cannot change
         the underlying table shape directly; schema changes
         land via additive-only migrations applied on boot
         (see migrations.additive-only), and are visible to
         all branches immediately on next function lookup.

         data_rows has no branch_id; type_defs are stored
         per-branch but the underlying table shape is shared
         (see object-model). Branch code that ignores new
         fields reads a subset without error. Branch code
         that expects removed fields would fail, but
         additive-only means removed fields do not occur.

         type_defs merge: not yet implemented. Today all
         data lives in the shared data_rows table; per-type
         Postgres tables are not created by deftype.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection personal-workspace
   (doc "Boot on empty <user>/personal; seed package defaults only onto system/main")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         MODEL:
         - system/main = package defaults (inheritance root).
           Boot seed_stdlib writes libs/*.pp here only,
           under seed-policy (additive — see below).
         - <user>/personal = garden (user channel).
           Empty child of system/main (parent pointer only).
           Image boots here after seed. User (define) and
           (route) write personal rows only.
         - <user>/dev = optional workshop (also empty child).
           Prefer for product spikes you intend to promote;
           keep personal for long-lived custom pins.
         - <parent>/agent = agent user's default workshop.
           Empty child of system/main (sibling of the parent
           human's personal). An agent user boots here, not into
           the parent's personal. The agent works in its own
           branch and promotes into the parent's personal when
           the human allows it (see promote).
         - Inheritance: recursive CTE; child overrides
           shadow main; unpinned names auto-see main updates.

         BOOT ORDER:
         1. ensure_branch owner=username name=personal
            parent=system/main (no code copy)
         2. seed_stdlib with store.branch_id = system/main
            (additive persist — see seed-policy)
         3. checkout personal + rehydrate env from CTE
         4. refresh ui/nav shell

         load-library on CURRENT branch still upserts that
         branch (intentional pin). On personal/dev that pins
         the whole package — prefer small overrides or work
         name-by-name via define.

         (checkout "system/main") / (checkout "admin/personal")
         rehydrate sync; header shows current branch.
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection unbind
   (doc "Remove a branch-local binding; reveal shadowed system/main, never delete through")
   (implements image/lib/store.ml image/lib/primitives.ml image/lib/session.ml)
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-05: implemented (unbind name) in Store.delete_function + session.ml prim. Tested on local dev: unbind branch-local override reveals system/main binding; unbind missing returns "nothing to unbind"; unbind on system/main errors. 160 dune tests green.|) *)
   (verify
    (build "dune build")
    (test "dune runtest — unbind cases")
    (test "(unbind name) on a branch-local define removes the branch row; system/main binding revealed")
    (test "(unbind name) on system/main errors (cannot unbind on main)")
    (test "(unbind name) with no branch-local row errors (nothing to unbind)")
    (test "unbind never deletes another branch or system/main rows"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         PROBLEM: transient defines accumulate as permanent litter —
         agent probes, test fns, spike code. Every (define) on a
         personal branch is a permanent row. There is no way to
         remove a branch-local binding without deleting the whole
         branch.

         PRIM: (unbind name)
           Deletes ONLY the current branch's function_defs row for
           name. After unbind, name resolves via inheritance to
           whatever system/main (or a parent branch) provides —
           revealing the shadowed binding, if any.

         SEMANTICS:
           - On <user>/personal or <user>/dev: deletes the
             (branch_id, name) row from function_defs. The env
             rehydrates so the name now resolves to the inherited
             (system/main) binding, or becomes unbound if main
             has no such name.
           - On system/main: ERROR. Unbind never deletes a
             system/main row. (system/main is the inheritance
             root; removing from it would affect every user.)
           - If no branch-local row exists for name: ERROR
             (nothing to unbind). This prevents accidental no-ops
             and makes the caller explicit.
           - Never deletes rows from other branches.
           - Routes (view_bindings) are out of scope for v1;
             unbind is function_defs only.

         INTERACTION WITH PACKAGE PINS:
           load-library upserts/pins package bindings onto the
           current branch. (unbind name) on a pinned binding is
           an unpin — it removes the branch-local override,
           revealing the system/main package binding. A future
           (unbind-package pkg) could drop a whole pin; out of
           scope for v1.

         AUTH: admin or the branch owner (consistent with checkout
         / promote gating). Anonymous sessions cannot unbind.

         NON-GOALS:
           - Unbinding routes (view_bindings) — follow-up.
           - (unbind-package pkg) bulk unpin — follow-up.
           - Unbind on system/main (always errors).
    |))
   )
  )

  (subsection package-updates
   (doc "Authority model: live Postgres Nopales, git kernel + vault, additive seed")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02: dual-era authority is operational — seed-policy additive, pp_sync vault, promote path, personal pins survive deploy. Matches AGENTS.md.|) *)
   (implements image/lib/session.ml image/lib/store.ml image/lib/loader.ml cli/bin/pp_sync.ml common/lib/branch.ml common/lib/manifest.ml common/lib/protocol.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         ==========================================================
         DUAL-ERA AUTHORITY (intentional, long-lived)
         ==========================================================
         Destination: stable OCaml core; most product change is
         live define/promote on the habitat. Until the core is
         calm, both surfaces exist — but each has one job.

         THREE CHANNELS:

         1. KERNEL — OCaml binary (HTTP, prims, loader, chrome).
            Source of truth: git. Update: push → build → restart.
            No branch involved.

         2. PACKAGE DEFAULTS — function_defs on system/main.
            Source of truth while running: Postgres system/main.
            Daily update path: promote from personal/dev
            (see promote). Boot seed is ADDITIVE only
            (see seed-policy): fills missing names from
            libs/*.pp; never overwrites an existing main row.
            Explicit reseed is the only git→main clobber.

         3. GARDEN / WORKSHOP PINS — rows on personal, dev, or
            other children. Never written by boot seed.
            Survive every deploy. Shadow main until unoverride
            or promote+unoverride.

         GIT'S TWO JOBS (do not mix):
           A. Kernel repo — authoritative for OCaml/migrations.
           B. Nopales vault — exported snapshots of packages/
              branches (backup, bootstrap, clone-to-laptop).
              Not the daily Nopales editor. See lisp-snapshot.

         DATA (data_rows) is never versioned by branch and
         never wiped by deploy. New package code must tolerate
         older row shapes or ship an explicit data migrator.

         DEFAULT WORK LOOP (product Nopales):
           work on personal or dev on the ONE habitat (prod)
           → dogfood against real shared data
           → promote selected names → system/main
           → optional snapshot/export into repo (vault)
           → optional unoverride so inheritance is clean
           Local dev.sh PG is kernel-lab only, not daily Nopales.

         UPDATE RULE (after additive seed):
           new name in libs, absent on main → appears on seed
           existing main name              → unchanged by seed
           personal/dev override           → still shadows main
           want git body on main           → explicit reseed
           want garden body on main        → promote

         CONSCIOUS UPDATE UX (branch browser; partial today):
           per name: Use main (unoverride) | Keep | Diff
           per package: Reset garden pins to main (bulk unoverride)
           load-library on non-main: warn that it pins the package

         Anti-patterns:
           - boot seed overwriting existing main defs
           - reseed onto personal/dev
           - auto-merge main→personal on boot
           - full copy-on-fork for personal (freezes forever)
           - treat data_rows as package payload
           - editing libs/*.pp as the primary product path
             while also defining the same names live without
             promote (split brain)
    |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build image cli")
    (smoke "boot seed does not change body of existing system/main fn")
    (smoke "boot seed inserts a libs name missing from system/main")
    (smoke "personal define survives image restart without pp_sync"))
  )
  (subsection seed-policy
   (doc "Additive boot seed into system/main; explicit reseed may clobber")
   (status implemented)
   (implements image/lib/session.ml image/lib/store.ml image/lib/loader.ml image/lib/primitives.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         PROBLEM TODAY:
         persist_function is INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE.
         seed_stdlib load-library therefore CLOBBERS every
         system/main name present in libs/*.pp on every boot.
         Live promote to main is not durable across deploy.

         OPTION A (chosen):

         1. ADDITIVE BOOT SEED
            seed_stdlib still runs on boot, still targets only
            system/main, still reads PP_LIBS_DIR/<pkg>/lib.pp.
            Persist path used by seed MUST NOT overwrite:
              INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (branch_id, name) DO NOTHING
            or equivalent "insert if absent" helper
            (Store.persist_function_if_absent / seed mode flag).
            Missing package files: skip + log (unchanged).
            Routes/deftype seeded from libs: same additive rule
            where a natural key exists (branch_id, route|name).

         2. EXPLICIT RESEED (git → main clobber, admin only)
            Prim: (reseed-package "notes")
              optional (reseed-package "notes" 'force) same.
            Behavior:
              - require admin (same gate as promote to system/*)
              - read libs/<pkg>/lib.pp via loader
              - eval/persist onto system/main with UPSERT
                (full overwrite for names in that file)
              - does not touch personal/dev rows
              - rehydrate callers as needed
            CLI: pp-sync reseed <pkg>  (calls the prim)
            Optional later: pp-sync reseed --all

         3. load-library / load-string (interactive)
            Unchanged intent: write the CURRENT branch with
            upsert. Still the pin hammer. Not used by boot
            seed once seed has its own absent-only persist.
            Warn when current branch is not system/main
            (package-updates UX; may land with change-set).

         4. FRESH DATABASE
            Additive seed on empty main = full bootstrap from
            libs/*.pp. No separate first-boot mode required.

         5. NON-GOALS
            - seed writing personal/dev
            - auto reseed on git push without admin prim
            - deleting main names absent from libs
              (additive never removes; cleanup is manual
               or a future prune tool)
    |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build image cli")
    (smoke "after promote name to main, restart leaves body intact")
    (smoke "(reseed-package pkg) admin overwrites main from libs")
    (smoke "non-admin reseed-package errors")
    (smoke "reseed-package does not write personal rows"))
  )
  (subsection promote
   (doc "Graduate garden/workshop code to system/main; optional vault export")
   (status implemented)
   (implements image/lib/session.ml image/lib/store.ml image/lib/http_branches.ml cli/bin/pp_sync.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Promote is the blessed path for product Nopales.
         Git export is optional vault/checkpoint, not required
         for main to keep the change across deploys (seed-policy).

         A. LIVE MAIN (required for shared defaults)
            Selective upsert of named function_defs from a
            source branch (personal/dev/…) → system/main.
            Already: Session.promote_to_main, Store.promote_functions,
            POST /branches/promote (admin only into system/*).
            Also: (merge src dst) for coarser branch merges;
            promote remains the selective UI/prim path.

            ROUTES TRAVEL WITH THEIR HANDLER:
            promote MUST also upsert any view_bindings row on
            the source whose handler names a promoted function
            (preserving visibility). A promoted handler without
            its route leaves main serving the old binding —
            the split-brain footgun this rule exists to kill.
            Routes whose handler is NOT in the promote set do
            not travel. promote does not touch type_defs
            (schema-authority).

         B. VAULT EXPORT (optional durability in git)
            export-package / pp-sync pull writes libs/<pkg>/lib.pp
            (or snapshot tree — see lisp-snapshot). Operator
            commits. This does NOT change live main by itself.
            Useful for backup, laptop clone, and explicit reseed.

         HAPPY PATH:
           edit on personal or dev → dogfood
           → promote selected names → system/main
           → optional unoverride on source (clean inheritance)
           → optional pp-sync snapshot/pull + git commit (vault)

         SELECTIVE:
           Ship named functions (ui/nav, notes/save, …),
           not the entire garden by default.
           UI: name multi-select on branch detail (exists);
           package-group select may come later.

         PRIM / CLI (v1 make):
           keep promote UI + Session.promote_to_main
           optional Nopales: (promote-to-main "name") or
             (promote-to-main '("n1" "n2")) on current branch
           pp-sync does not promote (image policy stays in image)

         Policy:
           - promote/merge into system/*: admin only
           - merge into own branches: free
           - data_rows never part of promote
           - image never git-pushes (no repo credentials)
    |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "POST /branches/promote copies selected names to system/main")
    (smoke "promoted main body survives restart under additive seed")
    (smoke "non-admin promote to system/main denied")
    (smoke "promote of a handler fn also copies its view_bindings row")
    (smoke "view_bindings whose handler is not promoted stay put"))
  )
  (subsection code-history
   (doc "Append-only before-image history for code rows, captured by Postgres triggers")
   (status implemented)
   (implements migrations/012_code_history.sql image/lib/store.ml image/lib/session.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         PROBLEM:
         Every write to function_defs / type_defs /
         view_bindings is a destructive INSERT ... ON CONFLICT
         DO UPDATE. Redefine a function and its old body is
         unrecoverable unless someone happened to pp-sync
         snapshot first. Merge and promote clobber the same
         way. There is no history anywhere in the system.

         DESIGN: HISTORY TABLES + TRIGGERS (capture below
         every write path)

           code_history(
             id         bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY,
             rel        text,          -- 'function_defs' | 'type_defs' | 'view_bindings'
             branch_id  uuid,
             name       text,          -- fn/type name or route path
             body       jsonb,         -- superseded before-image (fn/type body)
             author     text,
             doc        text,
             package    text,
             op         text,          -- 'UPDATE' | 'DELETE'
             changed_at timestamptz DEFAULT now()
           )
           index on (rel, branch_id, name, changed_at DESC)

         One BEFORE UPDATE OR DELETE FOR EACH ROW trigger per
         live table inserts OLD into code_history. Triggers,
         not OCaml call-site instrumentation, because every
         write path converges on these tables: define, merge,
         promote, reseed, boot seed, and even raw psql. A
         trigger cannot be bypassed by a new call site.

         TRANSACTIONALITY: the trigger runs in the same
         transaction as the write. A rolled-back merge leaves
         no history rows; a committed merge leaves one per
         clobbered row. History is therefore exact, never
         optimistic.

         SEMANTICS:
         - INSERT fires nothing: first definition has no
           before-image. Version N of a name = live row plus
           N-1 code_history entries walking changed_at DESC.
         - ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE fires the UPDATE trigger for
           conflicted rows only — merge/promote history is
           exactly the clobbered set, for free.
         - INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING (additive seed)
           writes nothing and records nothing.
         - op='DELETE' rows are how unoverride/branch-drop
           remain recoverable.

         NOPALES PRIMS (read-only):
           (code-history "name")            -- current branch
           (code-history "name" "owner/branch")
             → JSON list, newest first:
               {rel, op, author, doc, changed_at, body}
           (code-diff "name" n)             -- current row vs
             nth-newest history body (unified-ish text diff)
         Restore is NOT a prim in v1: read the old body and
         define it back (that define itself lands in history,
         so restore-by-redefine is lossless).

         SCOPE / NON-GOALS (v1):
         - CODE ROWS ONLY. data_rows history is a separate
           later decision (storage commitment; shared table).
         - No retention pruning; review table size later.
         - No history UI in v1 beyond the prims; the
           branch-browser Changes view may surface it later.
         - tree_nodes mirrors are derived state; history
           lives on the authoritative tables only.
    |))
   )
   (verify
    (test "image/test/history_test.ml")
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "redefine a fn on personal; (code-history name) returns the old body")
    (smoke "merge that clobbers target rows records one history row per clobber")
    (smoke "additive boot seed records no history rows")
    (smoke "rolled-back transaction leaves no history rows"))
  )
  (subsection land
   (doc "pp-sync land: promote + vault snapshot + git commit as one operator step")
   (status implemented)
   (implements cli/bin/pp_sync.ml scripts/pp-sync.sh cli/lib/pp_client.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         PROBLEM:
         The blessed loop (work on personal/dev → promote →
         snapshot → git commit) has manual steps 3–4. Skip
         them and live main drifts from libs/ silently;
         pp-sync status can see the drift but nothing runs it.

         LAND — the one-command ship path:
           pp-sync land <pkg> [name ...] [--from owner/branch]
         performs, in order, from the operator's laptop:
           1. CHECKOUT-INDEPENDENT READ: export the named
              functions (or the whole package when no names
              given) from the source branch (default: the
              session's current branch).
           2. PROMOTE: call the promote prim for those names
              (admin gate unchanged; routes travel per
              promote).
           3. SNAPSHOT: re-export the same names from
              system/main and write libs/<pkg>/lib.pp.
           4. COMMIT: git add libs/<pkg>/lib.pp && git commit
              with message "vault(<pkg>): land <names>".
              The image never git-pushes and never holds repo
              credentials; land runs where the repo lives.
           5. REPORT: print promoted names, conflicts the
              promote overwrote (visible via code-history),
              and the commit SHA.

         Failure stops the pipeline at the failed step and
         reports state (nothing after a failed promote is
         snapshotted; a failed commit leaves main promoted
         and the vault dirty, printed clearly).

         DRIFT VISIBILITY:
           pp-sync status gains an exit code: 0 clean,
           1 drift. The repo monitor command (README) runs
           pp-sync status so drift shows up on the dashboard
           instead of being discovered by accident.

         NON-GOALS:
           - land does not merge branches (promote only)
           - land does not push to remotes
           - land does not unoverride the source branch
             (stays an explicit, separate act)
           - no daemon; land is operator/agent-invoked
    |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build cli")
    (smoke "pp-sync land notes ui/nav promotes, snapshots libs/notes/lib.pp, commits")
    (smoke "failed promote (non-admin) leaves vault untouched and reports")
    (smoke "pp-sync status exits 1 when libs/ differs from live main"))
  )
  (subsection lisp-snapshot
   (doc "Clone habitat Nopales state into the git repo as vault/backup")
   (status implemented)
   (implements image/lib/session.ml image/lib/primitives.ml cli/bin/pp_sync.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         PURPOSE:
         Postgres is the live Nopales store. The repo must still
         be able to hold a clone of package/branch state for
         backup, new-machine bootstrap material, and explicit
         reseed inputs — without becoming the daily editor.

         V1 SURFACES (compose existing export):

         1. Package pull (exists)
            (export-package "notes") → lib.pp text
            pp-sync pull <pkg>       → libs/<pkg>/lib.pp
            pp-sync pull-all         → every discovered pkg
            Default export reads the SESSION's current branch
            visibility (CTE). For vault of defaults, checkout
            system/main first OR pass an explicit branch
            (v1 make: add optional branch ref).

         2. Snapshot command (new CLI)
            pp-sync snapshot
              - checkout-aware: export all packages with
                bindings on the chosen branch
              - writes libs/<pkg>/lib.pp by default (main vault)
            pp-sync snapshot --branch system/main
            pp-sync snapshot --branch admin/personal \
              --out backups/personal/
              - garden pins land under backups/ not libs/
                so bootstrap seed does not treat pins as defaults

         3. Image prim (thin)
            (export-package pkg) already returns text.
            Optional: (snapshot-packages) → list of pkg names
            with own or visible bindings (helper for CLI).
            No git commit inside the image.

         4. Operator habit
            Before risky reseed or migration: snapshot main
            (+ personal if desired) and git commit.
            Cadence backups may be cron + pp-sync snapshot.

         LAYOUT CONVENTION:
           libs/<pkg>/lib.pp     bootstrap + main vault (seed input)
           backups/<ref>/...     non-main branch dumps (not seeded)

         NON-GOALS v1:
           - automatic git commit from image or CLI
           - pg_dump of data_rows as part of pp-sync
           - encrypting snapshots
           - bidirectional sync daemon
    |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build cli")
    (smoke "pp-sync pull notes writes libs/notes/lib.pp")
    (smoke "pp-sync snapshot --branch system/main refreshes libs")
    (smoke "pp-sync snapshot --branch u/personal --out backups/personal does not write libs"))
  )
  (subsection branch-browser-ui
   (doc "Git-like light /branches browser: list, open, stats, checkout, promote")
   (status implemented)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-02: http_branches.ml ~710 lines — list with own code/overrides, detail, checkout, fork, promote to main + soft lint. Spec "TODAY bare table" is stale.|) *)
   (implements image/lib/http.ml image/lib/store.ml image/lib/session.ml)
   (details
    (doc (|
         TODAY: GET /branches is a bare table
         (owner/name, parent uuid prefix, created).
         No checkout, stats, detail, or promote.

         GOAL: tiny Gitea-ish branch page in classic
         htmx chrome — browse, open, stats, checkout,
         later promote — no SPA.

         ROUTES:
           GET  /branches
           GET  /branches?ref=owner/name   detail
           POST /branches/checkout         ref=…
           POST /branches/fork             from + name
           POST /branches/merge            src + dst (later)
           POST /branches/promote          selective (later)

         LIST PAGE:
           Header strip: On: <current> ★ + quick links
           Table columns:
             ★ | branch | parent | own code |
             overrides | routes | age | actions
           own code = COUNT function_defs WHERE branch_id
             (no CTE). overrides = own names also on parent.
           actions: Open · Checkout (disabled if current)
           Labels: system/main "defaults"; */personal "garden"
           Create card: fork-from select + new name + Create
             (full fork for experiments; personal stays empty)

         DETAIL PAGE (?ref=):
           Title, parent link, Checkout, back to list
           Stats: own fns, overrides, own routes,
             visible fn count (CTE), last define, authors
           Sections (htmx partials):
             Overrides (default for personal) — name,
               author, updated, new|override, view, diff
             All own code
             Routes (own view_bindings)
             Activity / Changes: branching.change-set
               (ORDER BY updated_at on own defs + transcript)
           Diff vs parent v1: two <pre> columns of pp_expr
             (identical / changed / only-here / only-parent)

         CHECKOUT:
           POST → Session.checkout_to → 303 /branches
           or HX-Redirect. Defines already persisted; no
           "uncommitted" confirm.

         PROMOTE PANEL (detail, admin):
           package multi-select + name multi-select
           [ ] Merge into system/main
           [ ] Mark for export (pp_sync pull list)
           Never dump whole garden by default.

         UPDATES BADGE (package-updates):
           override where main.updated_at > personal
           → "main newer" + Accept (unoverride) | Keep | Diff

         STORE HELPERS (own-branch, no CTE unless noted):
           count_functions_on_branch
           count_routes_on_branch
           list_own_function_names
           list_overrides (own ∩ parent names)
           lookup_function_on_branch (no CTE)
           delete_function_on_branch (unoverride)

         NON-GOALS (light):
           git SHAs/commits, PR review UI, blame,
           per-branch data, fancy fork-tree viz
    |))
   )
   (verify
    (build "dune build")
    (smoke "GET /branches shows current ★ and checkout")
    (smoke "POST checkout switches current-branch")
    (smoke "detail lists personal overrides vs main")
    (smoke "detail Changes section lists own defs by updated_at"))
  )
  (subsection change-set
   (doc "Branch change set / transcript: what changed on this branch")
   (status implemented)
   (implements image/lib/store.ml image/lib/http_branches.ml image/lib/session.ml)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "GET /changes?ref=u/personal lists own fns by updated_at")
    (smoke "(branch-changes) returns own function names + updated_at on current branch")
    (smoke "after define on personal, name appears at top of changes")
    (smoke "change row links to /edit and /bindings detail + parent diff when override"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         ==========================================================
         WHY (Smalltalk change set / method transcript)
         ==========================================================
         Combo pack item #2. After live defines, humans and agents
         need "what did I change on this garden?" without git.
         Not a full VCS — a living transcript of own-branch code
         rows + optional session ring of recent defines.

         Compounds with:
           - image.inspector impact-queue (same define hook)
           - image.system-browser detail → link here for overrides
           - branching.branch-browser-ui detail Activity section
           - promote / package-updates (know what is dirty)

         ==========================================================
         A. DERIVED CHANGE SET (no new table required v1)
         ==========================================================
         Source of truth already exists:
           Store.list_own_functions ~branch_id
             → (name, author, updated_at)
           Store.list_own_routes ~branch_id
           Store.list_overrides ~branch_id ~parent_id
             → new | override vs parent

         "Changes" for branch B =
           own function_defs ∪ own view_bindings on B,
           sorted by updated_at DESC (activity order).
         Classify each fn row:
           - new: no parent row of same name
           - override: parent has name (optionally note if
             parent.updated_at > child → main-newer badge)
           - route: own view_bindings entries (path → handler)

         Nopales prims (read-only, current branch default):
           (branch-changes) → list of dict/JSON strings
             {name, kind: fn|route, author, updated_at,
              class: new|override|route}
           (branch-changes "owner/name") optional other branch
             if visible to user (same rules as /branches detail)

         ==========================================================
         B. SESSION TRANSCRIPT (define hook)
         ==========================================================
         Same write path as impact-queue (persist_define /
         install_function success):
           append {name, at, branch, author, op: define|route|delete}
           to session ring (last N=64). Optional; derived list
           from PG is enough if ring is empty after restart.

         Nopales: (change-transcript) → recent session ops
         Do not require durable data_rows log in v1.

         ==========================================================
         C. UI SURFACES
         ==========================================================
         1) Branch detail (primary):
              GET /branches?ref=owner/name&view=changes
              or default Activity section expanded for personal
              Table: when | name | class | author | open | diff
              open → /bindings?pkg=&fn= or /edit/<name>
              diff → existing ?diff= two-column pp_expr

         2) Optional GET /changes on current branch (short alias
            redirecting to /branches?ref=<current>&view=changes)

         3) system-browser function detail: if name is own-row,
            badge "on this branch" + link into Changes highlight

         ==========================================================
         D. AGENT
         ==========================================================
         After a multi-define session, agent can (branch-changes)
         or read /changes to summarize dirty garden before promote.
         install_function result may append "changeset: N own fns"
         lightly — impact text remains primary for smoke routes.

         ==========================================================
         E. NON-GOALS
         ==========================================================
           - Per-edit revision history table (later; updated_at only)
           - git commits / SHAs / blame
           - Diff of data_rows (code only)
           - Cross-user global firehose
           - Blocking define on "uncommitted" state

         ==========================================================
         F. PHASES
         ==========================================================
         C1: UI Activity/Changes from list_own_* + overrides class
         C2: (branch-changes) prim + /changes alias
         C3: session transcript ring on define (shared hook w/ impact)
         C4: browser detail deep-link + agent one-liner

         SUCCESS: on personal after editing ui/nav, Changes shows
         ui/nav at top as override; diff vs main works; agent can
         list dirty names without scraping HTML.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection ast-nodes
   (doc "Design: addressable AST nodes as substrate for surgical edits and AI patches")
   (status planned)
   (* agent note (|2026-08-16 design pass (ralph loop 19): nodal addressing + three-way merge are now fixed; materialized nodes table deferred. Re-filed issue BORGE-1786925842. 2026-08-02: design-only proposal; function_defs remain whole-body JSONB.|) *)
   (details
    (doc (|
         TODAY: two trees, two models
         - Branch tree: rows in branches, fork/merge,
           recursive CTE lookup, unit = whole function
         - AST tree: JSONB blob in function_defs.body,
           in-memory eval walk, no node ids, edit =
           replace whole body

         DESIGN (2026-08-16, ralph loop 19, BORGE-1786925842):
         Give AST nodes ADDRESSABILITY so tools can patch
         subexpressions and a genuine THREE-WAY MERGE can be
         computed, reusing the diff spine already shipped
         (branching.diff / lisp-interpreter.ast-diff). Kept
         cheap: identity is a PATH computed on the fly, NOT a
         materialized table. A materialized nodes table stays
         OPTIONAL and DEFERRED (see non-goals).

         ========= 1. NODE ADDRESSING (no table) =========
         A node inside a whole-expr body is addressed by a
         PATH from the root, in the same vocabulary the
         ast_diff walk already labels its children with:
           positional: if.test if.then if.else
                       lambda.params lambda.body
                       let.name let.val let.body
                       define.name define.val
                       seq.a seq.b quote.body
           list:       app.fn app.arg[i] prim.arg[i]
                       letrec.binding[NAME] lambda.param[i]
         Example path: lambda.body.app.arg[1].app.fn.var
         Identity = (row key: branch_id, name) + path.
         Pure helpers in lisp/lib make nodes addressable with
         NO schema change (function bodies stay whole-expr
         JSONB):
           select_path  : expr -> path -> expr option
           replace_path : path -> expr -> expr -> expr

         ========= 2. PATCH REPRESENTATION =========
         A two-way diff (base -> target) flattens to a set of
         atomic path-keyed edits, exactly what Ast_diff.delta
         already produces labelled by the child paths above:
           Replace(path, from, to)   node at path replaced
           Insert(path, idx, expr)   list-child insert
           Delete(path, idx)         list-child remove
         The Replaced <from=absent> sentinel in ast_diff
         already encodes Insert/Delete at list positions. A
         patch is therefore diff(base,target) PLUS the path
         vocabulary - no new algorithm, only the missing
         path->node selector from part 1.

         ========= 3. THREE-WAY MERGE (base, ours, theirs)
                  the algorithm tree-time (campaign 5) and
                  local-first sync (campaign 8) both reuse
            =========
           ours   = diff(base, ours)
           theirs = diff(base, theirs)
         Group edits by node path. Disjoint paths compose
         with no cross-order. A same path edited by BOTH:
           - identical target  -> keep once (both agreed)
           - different target  -> CONFLICT: report path + ours
             value + theirs value; v1 resolution = keep-ours
             + report (consistent with branching.merge's
             report-only-conflict philosophy); v2 may offer
             3-way resolution.
         Apply the merged edits to base -> merged body, then
         re-validate (ast_hash equality / eval smoke). A
         merge always has a base: the fork point, branches.
         last_synced, or a code_history before-image.

         ========= 4. MATERIALIZED NODES TABLE (optional, deferred)
            =========
         For SURGICAL in-place patching of one huge function
         (AI rewrites a subexpression without rewriting the
         whole JSONB), a materialized
           nodes(id, branch_id, kind, payload, parent_id,
                 position, ...)
         table pays off - derived/mirrored from the expr on
         write (like the tree_nodes mirrors), keyed by
         (branch_id, name, path). DEFERRED: the non-goal
         below (whole-function propose/edit ships first) says
         it is not needed yet, and it adds schema + mirror +
         writer complexity.

         ========= 5. LAYERING =========
         P1 (this substrate): pure path addressing +
             replace_path + merge3 in lisp/lib, testable with
             dune runtest lisp (no Postgres).
         P2: wire into Store.merge_branch - replace the naive
             full-copy with diff-based merge + a conflict
             report using merge3 (this is branching.merge's
             open TODO).
         P3 (deferred): materialized nodes table for surgical
             in-place patching.

         IMPLEMENTED so far: the diff spine this design
         consumes (branching.diff + lisp-interpreter.ast-diff)
         is shipped (module ast_diff, /diff page, (diff from
         to) prim). This subsection fixes the AST-node
         addressing + merge3 design those consumers build on;
         the P1 merge3 module and P2 merge wire-in are the
         remaining make work (see BORGE-1786925842).

         Non-goals for first product cut (unchanged):
         - Do not block function-editor or ai-authoring
           tools on this redesign
         - Whole-function propose/edit ships first
         - No alpha-equivalence / rename detection in the
           merge (inherits the diff spine non-goals)
    |))
   )
  )
 )
)
